169
00:14:43,611 --> 00:14:46,833
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is craving involved, if there is pressure involved, means there are hindrances involved.

170
00:14:47,133 --> 00:14:50,454
[SPEAKER_02]: And if there are hindrances involved, there is no such thing as jhāna there for you.

171
00:14:53,416 --> 00:14:54,116
[SPEAKER_00]: That can be true.

172
00:14:54,676 --> 00:14:55,817
[SPEAKER_00]: I notice even

173
00:14:57,405 --> 00:15:00,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if your technique is not as, sort of...

174
00:15:00,966 --> 00:15:01,306
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

175
00:15:01,966 --> 00:15:02,986
[SPEAKER_01]: You know... Proliferated.

176
00:15:03,006 --> 00:15:09,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Proliferated as those, but you're still doing it with that same attitude that you don't recognize why you're even doing it.

177
00:15:09,328 --> 00:15:10,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

178
00:15:11,188 --> 00:15:20,291
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, that's why, like the Buddha said, you know, in jhāna, that the pleasure of jhāna is not something you can crave for, before or after, or while you're in it.

179
00:15:21,351 --> 00:15:24,031
[SPEAKER_02]: If there are no underlying tendencies, underline that.

180
00:15:24,832 --> 00:15:26,412
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why that pleasure is wholesome.

181
00:15:29,070 --> 00:15:42,395
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you enter jhāna by relinquishing craving, by relinquishing things you crave for, which, for most people who learn jhāna beforehand, would be relinquishing the idea of jhāna, relinquishing the idea of meditation technique leading to jhāna.

182
00:15:42,696 --> 00:15:45,637
[SPEAKER_02]: That would bring you closer to jhāna than doing the technique towards jhāna.

183
00:15:51,099 --> 00:15:58,162
[SPEAKER_02]: And once, yes, the mind abides in such relinquishing, relinquishment, withdrawn from unwholesome states,

184
00:15:59,367 --> 00:16:11,556
[SPEAKER_02]: In seclusion, then, as the Buddha said, yeah, whatever, whether in that state, or first jhāna, or second jhāna, and so on, whether I walk, eat, go to toilet, extend my arm, whatever I do, it's divine.

185
00:16:12,197 --> 00:16:14,238
[SPEAKER_02]: It's outside of the domain of the hindrances.

186
00:16:14,358 --> 00:16:14,859
[SPEAKER_02]: It's pure.

187
00:16:16,440 --> 00:16:17,461
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you live in jhāna.

188
00:16:17,481 --> 00:16:18,541
[SPEAKER_02]: You abide in that jhāna.

189
00:16:18,641 --> 00:16:25,487
[SPEAKER_01]: That's because you've relinquished the body that is the necessary basis for all of the hindrances.

190
00:16:25,687 --> 00:16:26,868
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the hindrances

191
00:16:28,965 --> 00:16:30,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

192
00:16:30,386 --> 00:16:30,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

193
00:16:30,846 --> 00:16:33,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

194
00:16:35,309 --> 00:16:43,633
[SPEAKER_02]: And that obviously kind of, you know, calls for redefining hindrances of... statement hindrances affect the body.

195
00:16:44,173 --> 00:16:45,094
[SPEAKER_02]: I know what you mean, but...

196
00:16:45,754 --> 00:16:50,577
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you have not surmounted the hindrances,

197
00:16:51,658 --> 00:17:05,081
[SPEAKER_02]: Your idea of hindrances affecting the body would be very different than if you have surmounted the hindrances, and then you can say, yes, you know, hindrances apply to the body, but there's obviously a very different level of how affected the body is for somebody who is free from hindrances than somebody who isn't.

198
00:17:05,701 --> 00:17:08,782
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where, in our last talk, we talked about authenticity.

199
00:17:08,902 --> 00:17:13,763
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where it comes in, really, like seeing, is this rooted in craving?

200
00:17:14,203 --> 00:17:14,423
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

201
00:17:14,683 --> 00:17:17,163
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like really being self-honest.

202
00:17:17,783 --> 00:17:20,264
[SPEAKER_02]: You might catch yourself and you realize, okay, this is

203
00:17:21,150 --> 00:17:22,290
[SPEAKER_02]: This is rooted in craving.

204
00:17:22,330 --> 00:17:29,933
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, I'm here craving against these hindrances in order to re-establish myself in jhāna, which is why I'm within hindrances.

205
00:17:30,273 --> 00:17:32,074
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of my craving, not because of the hindrances.

206
00:17:33,054 --> 00:17:33,935
[SPEAKER_02]: So now you recognize that.

207
00:17:33,955 --> 00:17:35,335
[SPEAKER_02]: That doesn't mean, okay, so I'll stop this.

208
00:17:35,395 --> 00:17:35,695
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no.

209
00:17:35,715 --> 00:17:43,278
[SPEAKER_02]: It means like, okay, no, I detected the mistake, so all I should do now is still strive to be free from hindrances, just minus my craving for being free from hindrances.

210
00:17:46,510 --> 00:17:49,692
[SPEAKER_02]: And that brings us to that whole point that we often talk about.

211
00:17:49,792 --> 00:17:51,593
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, so then you learn how to endure it rightly.

212
00:17:52,494 --> 00:17:56,476
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you endure it rightly, sufficiently enough, you will be free from it.

213
00:17:57,277 --> 00:18:05,522
[SPEAKER_02]: It's your craving, while you're enduring things, whether you want it or not, it's your craving that interferes and obscures and blurs that picture.

214
00:18:06,923 --> 00:18:14,268
[SPEAKER_02]: So you might endure things for decades, but if you endure with your craving that you're unaware of, that endurance will not bear fruit.

215
00:18:15,397 --> 00:18:17,958
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you recognize, okay, yeah, endurance is necessary.

216
00:18:18,438 --> 00:18:20,059
[SPEAKER_02]: However, craving is not necessary.

217
00:18:20,959 --> 00:18:25,940
[SPEAKER_02]: I can stop craving to, you know, either have jhāna or be free from this unwholesome state.

218
00:18:26,261 --> 00:18:27,881
[SPEAKER_02]: I can just endure it without acting out of it.

219
00:18:28,241 --> 00:18:29,742
[SPEAKER_02]: By body, speech and mind.

220
00:18:30,402 --> 00:18:31,862
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you free yourself from hindrances.

221
00:18:32,302 --> 00:18:34,803
[SPEAKER_02]: And see, that's not underlined by craving now anymore.

222
00:18:36,124 --> 00:18:37,964
[SPEAKER_02]: The result of that will be free from craving.

223
00:18:38,685 --> 00:18:39,905
[SPEAKER_02]: And the result of that is jhāna.

224
00:18:43,750 --> 00:18:50,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the... because I really like the simile of... or the analogy of addiction, of being... kind of freeing yourself from addiction.

225
00:18:50,995 --> 00:18:54,417
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know... is this... is this a useful analogy?

226
00:18:54,437 --> 00:19:07,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you want to be free from the addiction of... addiction to cigarettes or whatever, it's not going to work if you sort of really just want the... the end results of kind of

227
00:19:08,212 --> 00:19:19,841
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to be, sort of, wanting to actually do the work of not giving in to continually smoking cigarettes.

228
00:19:20,761 --> 00:19:27,246
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you can't, sort of, expect to do something else that will then allow you to not be addicted to cigarettes.

229
00:19:27,266 --> 00:19:27,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

230
00:19:27,406 --> 00:19:29,628
[SPEAKER_02]: So, theoretically, analogy is right.

231
00:19:30,048 --> 00:19:31,990
[SPEAKER_02]: But it doesn't give you the...

232
00:19:32,150 --> 00:19:34,952
[SPEAKER_02]: Practically, what a person needs to see is, like, it's not enough

233
00:19:36,191 --> 00:19:39,914
[SPEAKER_02]: When it comes to sensuality, to not just engage with sensuality physically.

234
00:19:40,614 --> 00:19:48,460
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, in case of cigarettes, all you need to do is physically stop smoking it and then the body will detox itself and you will not crave for cigarettes anymore.

235
00:19:48,500 --> 00:19:52,463
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, in case of sensuality, that same craving goes far beyond body.

236
00:19:52,503 --> 00:19:56,006
[SPEAKER_02]: It's bodily, of course, but it's rooted in your mind.

237
00:19:56,926 --> 00:20:01,970
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you will need to not engage with the body when it comes to sensuality.

238
00:20:02,490 --> 00:20:04,012
[SPEAKER_02]: You will need to not engage with speech.

239
00:20:04,895 --> 00:20:14,946
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, you need to be enduring it on the mental level, without acting out towards it, even by means of, like, distraction, and so on.

240
00:20:16,207 --> 00:20:25,317
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you endure it long enough on the mental level, without acting out towards it, one way or the other, that's how you will then uproot it in your mind as well.

241
00:20:25,894 --> 00:20:43,428
[SPEAKER_02]: But what happens is, people would be withdrawn in the body, in the speech, but then mentally, they would feel justified then, in doing all sorts of things, including their practice, because they don't want to deal with that pure pain of saying no to sensuality, without replacing it or covering it up.

242
00:20:44,265 --> 00:21:07,194
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what happens when somebody, like, becomes sense-restrained and celibate, and then they start doing all this work and projects and seemingly meaningful and engaging in ideas and all of that to keep themselves occupied and floating, because if they stop distracting themselves in that engaging manner, externally wholesome manner, well, if they stop doing it, then internally they would feel the pain on that mental, and that's what you need to feel.

243
00:21:08,419 --> 00:21:14,544
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean, like, even practicing the dhamma, doing things, so to not deal with the pain, is not the dhamma.

244
00:21:16,666 --> 00:21:19,688
[SPEAKER_02]: Dhamma is on the level of enduring it and not distracting yourself from it.

245
00:21:20,869 --> 00:21:26,133
[SPEAKER_00]: You can even, like, I don't know, read a sutta about freedom from sensuality.

246
00:21:26,374 --> 00:21:28,275
[SPEAKER_02]: Or do a subha, subha, that's the common one.

247
00:21:28,615 --> 00:21:31,618
[SPEAKER_02]: I must do a subha, I must do a subha, because I don't want to endure the pain of this.

248
00:21:32,118 --> 00:21:36,802
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, you must do a subha, but not at the expense of enduring the central pressure.

249
00:21:39,351 --> 00:21:51,246
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, exactly, if you don't habitually just try to get rid of that pain of central pressure and sensuality, you can do a Supa at any given time within that sensuality.

250
00:21:51,286 --> 00:21:57,994
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what Buddha said, you would be able, a monk who has developed his mind correctly, would be able to see ugly within beautiful.

251
00:21:58,949 --> 00:22:01,550
[SPEAKER_02]: Or beautiful within ugly, or ugly within ugly, whatever.

252
00:22:01,770 --> 00:22:04,411
[SPEAKER_02]: Permutation, he sees it right there as it is.

253
00:22:04,431 --> 00:22:15,015
[SPEAKER_02]: He wouldn't need to engage in a set of motions, techniques, and steps, and, you know, psychological methods to now replace this sensuality with a subha.

254
00:22:15,516 --> 00:22:18,517
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, that's rooted in craving, and craving is the root of sensuality.

255
00:22:19,417 --> 00:22:24,239
[SPEAKER_02]: So, although you have nullified the currently attractive object of your sensuality,

256
00:22:25,005 --> 00:22:29,808
[SPEAKER_02]: The whole framework of central pressure and pull and leaning towards those objects remains unchanged.

257
00:22:30,428 --> 00:22:41,615
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly what happened to the monks that did not develop yoniso, manasikāra sufficiently, and were trying to do this as subha, they ended up being attracted to corpses and and so on that Sutta has mentioned.

258
00:22:42,355 --> 00:22:43,676
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I keep saying.

259
00:22:43,696 --> 00:22:52,161
[SPEAKER_02]: The most important thing is knowledge, clarity, honesty in regard to the intentions behind your actions, whatever those actions might be, body, speech or mind.

260
00:22:53,002 --> 00:22:53,542
[SPEAKER_02]: So, here we are.

261
00:22:53,562 --> 00:22:54,563
[SPEAKER_02]: I have a central pressure.

262
00:22:55,792 --> 00:22:56,172
[SPEAKER_02]: What do you do?

263
00:22:56,232 --> 00:23:01,233
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, let me... let me spend next six hours reading the suttas and doing walking meditation.

264
00:23:01,633 --> 00:23:01,853
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

265
00:23:02,933 --> 00:23:05,934
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not that it's bad, but it's not good either.

266
00:23:05,974 --> 00:23:06,854
[SPEAKER_02]: Depends on why you do it.

267
00:23:07,254 --> 00:23:10,475
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you doing it so you don't have to feel the pain of sensuality?

268
00:23:10,495 --> 00:23:15,236
[SPEAKER_02]: Or are you doing it because you already saw the way out of sensuality and now you just want to fortify that?

269
00:23:15,336 --> 00:23:15,876
[SPEAKER_02]: Which one is it?

270
00:23:16,596 --> 00:23:21,057
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you think that just walking up and down and reading the suttas will magically give you a cure for sensuality?

271
00:23:22,638 --> 00:23:29,044
[SPEAKER_02]: Or do you just think, oh, no, I just need some more information to know how to deal with this purely, without resisting it, kicking, screaming, or trying to get rid of it.

272
00:23:29,764 --> 00:23:32,387
[SPEAKER_02]: So, your intention will determine whether you're doing it rightly or not.

273
00:23:36,430 --> 00:23:41,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Another word I've heard you use for intention is movement.

274
00:23:42,015 --> 00:23:44,758
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's helped me in the past, too.

275
00:23:45,138 --> 00:23:47,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, contemplate on the nature of intention.

276
00:23:48,821 --> 00:23:49,342
[SPEAKER_01]: For example,

277
00:23:50,820 --> 00:23:52,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'm speaking to you now.

278
00:23:52,920 --> 00:23:56,361
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm choosing to speak to you.

279
00:23:57,261 --> 00:24:01,122
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is this sort of intentional intention.

280
00:24:02,883 --> 00:24:13,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why that is, yeah, I've used the word intentional intention, is because that choice is contained within a broader

281
00:24:14,351 --> 00:24:20,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Intention, for example, of my heart beating, my brain functioning, my lungs breathing, that allow me to speak to you.

282
00:24:21,036 --> 00:24:21,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

283
00:24:21,277 --> 00:24:25,520
[SPEAKER_02]: A necessary basis for you in engaging, moving along those intentions.

284
00:24:25,580 --> 00:24:41,633
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this yoniso, manasikāra, you talk about, eh... yeah, one way of, sort of, reflecting on one's intentions is, like, are my intentions causing me to ignore this broader context which... which, eh...

285
00:24:42,618 --> 00:24:48,065
[SPEAKER_01]: It helps me to understand that, yeah, these choices I'm making, I don't own.

286
00:24:48,705 --> 00:24:59,098
[SPEAKER_02]: The point is, like, to recognize, like, of course, you can't, at the same time, to the same extent, think about the, you know, your current situation of beating heart, body here, any intention you're engaging with.

287
00:24:59,178 --> 00:25:00,420
[SPEAKER_02]: They will take some attention.

288
00:25:00,580 --> 00:25:01,341
[SPEAKER_02]: That's inevitable.

289
00:25:02,175 --> 00:25:08,278
[SPEAKER_02]: But, what is not inevitable, is you losing perspective, on account of choosing to act out of those intentions.

290
00:25:08,418 --> 00:25:09,358
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what you recognize.

291
00:25:09,398 --> 00:25:13,700
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so, like now, you have a intention to ask me a question.

292
00:25:14,660 --> 00:25:21,343
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, yeah, maybe that intention is rooted because I'm restless, or because I don't want to be quiet, or because I'm curious, or because I'm bored.

293
00:25:22,184 --> 00:25:24,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, obviously, in this case, or because I want to know.

294
00:25:24,845 --> 00:25:28,566
[SPEAKER_02]: Either of those ways will be engaging, to some extent.

295
00:25:29,047 --> 00:25:29,407
[SPEAKER_02]: And...

296
00:25:30,279 --> 00:25:40,127
[SPEAKER_02]: But as long as you now know, okay, but see, if I'm if I am asking it because I want to know, the answer can then shed more light for the future practice and context.

297
00:25:40,727 --> 00:25:48,613
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in that case, like pursuit of information is wholesome, because it can help you then eventually stop depending on external information.

298
00:25:49,394 --> 00:25:50,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Because sometimes people kind of

299
00:25:51,339 --> 00:25:58,661
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, get the sense of that, but then, too soon, they stop asking questions, because I mustn't ask questions, there will just be information and intellectual knowledge.

300
00:25:58,861 --> 00:26:01,101
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, you need to know what you're supposed to do.

301
00:26:02,022 --> 00:26:05,342
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, you will be engaged, and to that extent, you will lose perspective.

302
00:26:06,303 --> 00:26:15,425
[SPEAKER_02]: But, see, this course of action, loss of perspective, can then result in broadening perspective later on, because now you know what to look for, you will know what to notice, you might then notice it.

303
00:26:16,969 --> 00:26:28,405
[SPEAKER_02]: Going a different course of action, of not asking questions, or asking questions for wrong reasons, and not paying attention to it, well, again, that's also distracting itself, but then, in the future as well, will perpetuate the distraction, and it will not broaden the context.

304
00:26:29,148 --> 00:26:32,509
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it comes down to, yeah, why do I, like, why do I want to do this?

305
00:26:32,569 --> 00:26:39,191
[SPEAKER_02]: And often, like, people might be asking me questions on dhamma, but then they keep asking the same thing every time we meet.

306
00:26:40,311 --> 00:26:47,333
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, it's obviously apparent to me, but it might not still be apparent to them that, well, you're not doing this for the right reasons anymore.

307
00:26:47,373 --> 00:26:50,554
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just repeating the same thing because you need emotional conviction.

308
00:26:51,074 --> 00:26:53,675
[SPEAKER_02]: You need emotional kind of reassurance.

309
00:26:53,975 --> 00:26:56,895
[SPEAKER_02]: You are not merely seeking information on account of which to practice.

310
00:26:57,196 --> 00:26:58,676
[SPEAKER_02]: You want me to reassure you.

311
00:27:00,353 --> 00:27:01,374
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's not going to happen.

312
00:27:01,634 --> 00:27:11,503
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if I say things you want to hear, it's your own mind that's making you insecure and doubtful, which means it's going to last only for a period of time before it comes back.

313
00:27:11,663 --> 00:27:11,784
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

314
00:27:13,225 --> 00:27:26,297
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, yes, initially, the questions might have been rooted in really wanting to learn, but then that got obscured for yourself, and now your questions are rooted just in trying to rekindle that inspiration on account of very wise words that you heard externally.

315
00:27:26,766 --> 00:27:28,768
[SPEAKER_02]: Using you as some kind of pillar of strength.

316
00:27:29,468 --> 00:27:30,849
[SPEAKER_02]: And like, yeah, you might do that.

317
00:27:30,909 --> 00:27:36,354
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not necessarily an immediate clear-cut, but sooner or later, you will know clearly what you're doing.

318
00:27:36,394 --> 00:27:45,261
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's then when it comes down to, okay, so now, are you consciously, volitionally, choosing to perpetuate it, or are you trying to put a stop to it?

319
00:27:45,421 --> 00:27:46,362
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you acting out of it?

320
00:27:46,422 --> 00:27:46,882
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

321
00:27:46,902 --> 00:27:47,823
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you acting out of it?

322
00:27:48,784 --> 00:27:54,769
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's when the kind of choice of practicing the dhamma is brought back down onto you.

323
00:27:58,300 --> 00:28:01,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is the opportunity of seeing anicca.

324
00:28:01,341 --> 00:28:01,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

325
00:28:01,921 --> 00:28:07,222
[SPEAKER_02]: That is the opportunity of like converging in the right direction and reinforcing the right direction.

326
00:28:10,143 --> 00:28:12,364
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's entirely up to you.

327
00:28:12,844 --> 00:28:13,944
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the point.

328
00:28:13,964 --> 00:28:14,184
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

329
00:28:14,304 --> 00:28:17,225
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like, oh, I missed the chance or I slipped there.

330
00:28:17,245 --> 00:28:17,825
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't see this.

331
00:28:17,845 --> 00:28:18,185
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no.

332
00:28:18,606 --> 00:28:26,868
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always on the level of a very conscious choice that you, when you become aware of it, decide to either ignore or do something about it.

333
00:28:27,706 --> 00:28:45,679
[SPEAKER_01]: This also brings me back to the question I asked earlier on about... it's like sitting in a hall full of people, because I used to do that a lot, and there was always this sense of... this might be a bit off-topic, but the sense of there being this collective responsibility.

334
00:28:45,879 --> 00:28:46,279
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

335
00:28:46,319 --> 00:28:47,881
[SPEAKER_01]: We're doing this, like, you know, together.

336
00:28:47,961 --> 00:28:52,044
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, you got to abandon that long, long before you even start.

337
00:28:52,064 --> 00:28:54,685
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why seclusion is so important, being alone.

338
00:28:54,966 --> 00:28:56,126
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, this is...

339
00:28:57,088 --> 00:28:58,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Individual responsibility.

340
00:28:58,533 --> 00:29:00,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but being alone...

341
00:29:02,188 --> 00:29:04,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Mentally, I think, as well, as in not... Yeah.

342
00:29:05,010 --> 00:29:09,153
[SPEAKER_02]: Being alone with your own choices and recognizing there is no collective group here.

343
00:29:09,193 --> 00:29:09,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

344
00:29:09,714 --> 00:29:09,954
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

345
00:29:09,974 --> 00:29:10,074
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

346
00:29:10,094 --> 00:29:10,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

347
00:29:10,995 --> 00:29:15,218
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if you're sort of engaging with somebody, asking questions or... Yeah.

348
00:29:15,378 --> 00:29:16,979
[SPEAKER_02]: You still realize where the emphasis is.

349
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:17,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

350
00:29:17,220 --> 00:29:22,444
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to be doing it ultimately in order to be not needing it.

351
00:29:22,644 --> 00:29:22,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

352
00:29:23,305 --> 00:29:23,505
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

353
00:29:23,945 --> 00:29:29,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you kind of end up wanting to do... What you described, that often happens in the monasteries as well.

354
00:29:30,250 --> 00:29:37,236
[SPEAKER_02]: Monks develop this notion of we are in this together, type of thing, and it's it's it's absolutely laughable in a way.

355
00:29:37,676 --> 00:29:41,299
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, from the authenticity point of view, thinking we are doing this together, it's just, what?

356
00:29:41,880 --> 00:29:46,023
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I was saying that in some other context, you know, like the the assembly of arahants.

357
00:29:46,063 --> 00:29:46,824
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not an assembly.

358
00:29:46,844 --> 00:29:48,325
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just a group of individuals.

359
00:29:48,425 --> 00:29:51,168
[SPEAKER_02]: It's an external group of every single individual for himself.

360
00:29:51,448 --> 00:29:52,929
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's the purest assembly.

361
00:29:53,310 --> 00:29:53,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

362
00:29:54,250 --> 00:29:57,233
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no sense of projected identity in the group.

363
00:29:58,325 --> 00:30:00,446
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on the level of inauthenticity.

364
00:30:00,506 --> 00:30:04,928
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the level of, oh, it's easier for me to do this if everybody else around me is doing this.

365
00:30:05,528 --> 00:30:09,610
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that can be a factual kind of motive to start with.

366
00:30:09,730 --> 00:30:10,030
[SPEAKER_02]: Fine.

367
00:30:10,150 --> 00:30:10,430
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

368
00:30:10,950 --> 00:30:12,591
[SPEAKER_02]: Get reminded of what you're supposed to do.

369
00:30:13,265 --> 00:30:16,287
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't see a problem with that, it means you're never going to grow out of it.

370
00:30:16,487 --> 00:30:16,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

371
00:30:16,667 --> 00:30:22,230
[SPEAKER_02]: It means you will need, then, everybody else to keep doing the same thing, so you keep your peace of mind intact.

372
00:30:22,770 --> 00:30:33,797
[SPEAKER_02]: If one person steps out of the line, you will be existentially challenged, because it reminds you of the fact that, yeah, we are all doing the same now, but at any given moment, any given person can choose to do the opposite.

373
00:30:34,737 --> 00:30:35,978
[SPEAKER_01]: And that then means, oh, but then...

374
00:30:36,818 --> 00:30:37,958
[SPEAKER_02]: I can choose to do the opposite.

375
00:30:38,058 --> 00:30:45,440
[SPEAKER_02]: It means I'm not in control of this, you know, heavily controlled mind otherwise, and the environment and circumstances and the rules and observances that we all adhere to.

376
00:30:46,440 --> 00:30:47,260
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, there you go.

377
00:30:47,600 --> 00:30:48,861
[SPEAKER_02]: Your peace went out of the window.

378
00:30:49,701 --> 00:30:51,121
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm the owner of my actions.

379
00:30:51,301 --> 00:30:51,541
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

380
00:30:54,462 --> 00:30:55,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Alone with my actions.

381
00:30:56,982 --> 00:31:00,323
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, there's nothing and no one can come between you and your actions.

